            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "I don’t have live web access, so I can’t verify today’s very latest headlines. As of my latest reliable information, NASA’s **Perseverance rover** was still active in **Jezero Crater**, investigating rocks and sediments that may preserve evidence of ancient habitable environments on Mars.\n\nRecent key developments included:\n\n- **Exploring ancient river and delta deposits** in Jezero Crater, where water once flowed billions of years ago.\n- **Collecting and caching rock-core samples** for a future Mars Sample Return mission.\n- Studying areas such as **Neretva Vallis** and “Bright Angel,” which are scientifically interesting because they may contain altered rocks linked to past water activity.\n- NASA was also reassessing the **Mars Sample Return** architecture to reduce cost and complexity while still aiming to bring Perseverance’s samples back to Earth.\n\nFor the most current updates, the best sources are NASA’s official Perseverance mission page or NASA/JPL news releases.",
